{"w":2400,"h":3209,"lines":[{"c":1,"y":0.041936946425741861,"x":0.095051044163672452,"w":0.058441600799560553,"t":"Test 1","h":0.01496331639750359},{"w":0.75558420817057281,"x":0.13582457066714795,"y":0.086510942368446253,"h":0.022105827212705043,"c":1,"t":"lead to explosions. The intergovernmental groups also advise lowering satellites"},{"h":0.023908613923925515,"w":0.74991190592447909,"c":1,"t":"deep enough into the atmosphere that they will burn up or disintegrate within 25","y":0.10455905054512626,"x":0.139641309198661},{"h":0.021754897643472493,"c":1,"t":"years. But so far, only about half of all missions have abided by this 25-year goal,","y":0.12439079923399232,"x":0.13775252148748385,"w":0.76718989054361963},{"w":0.74407531738281252,"t":"says Holger Krag, head of the European Space Agency's space-debris office in","x":0.13960955147684159,"y":0.14055878781823394,"c":1,"h":0.025130041960243821},{"h":0.022469553249275909,"c":1,"t":"Darmstadt, Germany. Operators of the planned large constellations of satellites say","y":0.16136273960094594,"w":0.78087788899739585,"x":0.14160404824401496},{"c":1,"w":0.74398971557617188,"h":0.024802218048000646,"t":"they will be responsible stewards in their enterprises in space, but Krag worries","x":0.13966420212939776,"y":0.17890386988445428},{"c":1,"t":"that problems could increase, despite their best intentions. 'What happens to those","x":0.13966346415920222,"w":0.77894165039062502,"h":0.023781457348404644,"y":0.19887101715235633},{"h":0.023504630353220479,"y":0.21693894841973793,"c":1,"x":0.13966363368337104,"w":0.77113901774088522,"t":"that fail or go bankrupt?' he asks. 'They are probably not going to spend money to"},{"t":"remove their satellites from space.'","x":0.13980583102531161,"h":0.017496196354661042,"y":0.23831775709890912,"w":0.33203882853190103,"c":1},{"x":0.13958932981965244,"w":0.74615208943684908,"y":0.26405076157320928,"c":1,"h":0.02606019112179947,"t":"In theory, given the vastness of space, satellite operators should have plenty of"},{"y":0.28334529900502081,"x":0.13964721447786607,"t":"room for all these missions to fly safely without ever nearing another object. So","w":0.74400243123372389,"h":0.023112212088991835,"c":1},{"w":0.76146881103515629,"c":1,"t":"some scientists are tackling the problem of space junk by trying to find out where","x":0.14159548683286005,"h":0.022021650748089483,"y":0.30292434251900069},{"h":0.021788062930478524,"c":1,"t":"all the debris is to a high degree of precision. That would alleviate the need for","x":0.14161418640318402,"w":0.73812830607096358,"y":0.32108084877036691},{"w":0.71680857340494786,"h":0.02231597900390625,"y":0.33984561099245814,"x":0.1415862516744327,"c":1,"t":"many of the unnecessary manoeuvres that are carried out to avoid potential"},{"y":0.35930094428848214,"w":0.74971532185872403,"t":"collisions. 'If you knew precisely where everything was, you would almost never","h":0.021165808561806387,"c":1,"x":0.14165182570476142},{"t":"have a problem,' says Marlon Sorge, a space-debris specialist at the Aerospace","y":0.377114917945095,"w":0.74970520019531262,"c":1,"h":0.022441590686453461,"x":0.14166364124398106},{"c":1,"h":0.021890658084477321,"y":0.39765770570504633,"w":0.35957514444986982,"t":"Corporation in El Segundo, California.","x":0.14349201745580406},{"x":0.1416079759176381,"w":0.78863906860351562,"t":"The field is called space traffic management, because it's similar to managing traffic","y":0.42425346607819669,"h":0.023089713693779323,"c":1},{"w":0.75935923258463534,"c":1,"x":0.14360462299792412,"t":"on the roads or in the air. Think about a busy day at an airport, says Moriba Jah,","y":0.44324304442539697,"h":0.021086353527793977},{"h":0.021224925674010575,"y":0.46177570116217403,"t":"an astrodynamicist at the University of Texas at Austin: planes line up in the sky,","c":1,"x":0.14356164786827,"w":0.75166539510091146},{"y":0.47982643748419007,"w":0.76335103352864586,"c":1,"x":0.13966874315911695,"h":0.023632238512841353,"t":"landing and taking off close to one another in a carefully choreographed routine."},{"c":1,"t":"Air-traffic controllers know the location of the planes down to one metre in accuracy.","y":0.50057062276135278,"h":0.018541277828989067,"w":0.7865050760904948,"x":0.14363104884717814},{"y":0.51885024201591157,"t":"The same can't be said for space debris. Not all objects in orbit are known, and","h":0.02081559083172102,"x":0.14358798105882234,"w":0.74389460245768235,"c":1},{"t":"even those included in databases are not tracked consistently.","x":0.14361160266496578,"y":0.53901663014556467,"h":0.017978764471606623,"w":0.58654027303059897,"c":1},{"t":"An additional problem is that there is no authoritative catalogue that accurately","y":0.56552570740386432,"c":1,"x":0.13969175262363043,"w":0.74202402750651042,"h":0.022656297535168402},{"c":1,"x":0.14160725453616607,"t":"lists the orbits of all known space debris. Jah illustrates this with a web-based","y":0.58433890968997348,"w":0.73041625976562508,"h":0.023175127261152895},{"x":0.14354179271970974,"h":0.024060923733815298,"c":1,"y":0.60359054779228516,"t":"database that he has developed. It draws on several sources, such as catalogues","w":0.76734232584635431},{"y":0.62233562390169517,"w":0.77119288126627616,"h":0.023322226028204662,"c":1,"x":0.1435421647218568,"t":"maintained by the US and Russian governments, to visualise where objects are in"},{"h":0.021499177451445584,"c":1,"t":"space. When he types in an identifier for a particular space object, the database","x":0.14355611741709207,"y":0.64163454772307815,"w":0.7556019592285157},{"w":0.77694722493489565,"x":0.14356886139841749,"c":1,"y":0.65950428167543063,"t":"draws a purple line to designate its orbit. Only this doesn't quite work for a number","h":0.021959408198561681},{"t":"of objects, such as a Russian rocket body designated in the database as object","w":0.74985239664713532,"y":0.67850511064860475,"x":0.14547399682745368,"c":1,"h":0.022775154470283332},{"y":0.69735707758618348,"x":0.14548672856999417,"w":0.78672358194986969,"t":"number 32280. When Jah enters that number, the database draws two purple lines:","h":0.02340175830686575,"c":1},{"t":"the US and Russian sources contain two completely different orbits for the same","h":0.020881892884631825,"w":0.75947881062825529,"x":0.14356466541129048,"y":0.71588405261322929,"c":1},{"t":"object. Jah says that it is almost impossible to tell which is correct, unless a third","x":0.14547374767404336,"h":0.023809088353427321,"y":0.73473540478617583,"w":0.75762013753255197,"c":1},{"h":0.018627740735205567,"w":0.53801300048828116,"c":1,"x":0.14554102618635631,"t":"source of information made it possible to cross-correlate.","y":0.75565642902681796},{"t":"Jah describes himself as a space environmentalist: 'I want to make space a place","y":0.78251794252233975,"x":0.14554750895720739,"w":0.76714894612630213,"h":0.01925388466903355,"c":1},{"t":"that is safe to operate, that is free and useful for generations to come.' Until that","x":0.14540693206824518,"y":0.7983447139893719,"w":0.75772359212239571,"h":0.026518663067684023,"c":1},{"h":0.023056334498515407,"w":0.77701497395833341,"c":1,"y":0.81914933866087591,"x":0.14549129833033797,"t":"happens, he argues, the space community will continue devolving into a tragedy in"},{"x":0.14553864385767951,"h":0.019373610309351252,"w":0.60987879435221348,"y":0.84040374410455587,"c":1,"t":"which all spaceflight operators are polluting a common resource."},{"x":0.10097087388221566,"c":1,"h":0.01308139551465759,"y":0.95930232543902427,"w":0.025242718060811367,"t":"26"}]}